==English==

===Etymology===
A twentieth-century borrowing of {{etyl|grc}} {{term|ἐμπάθεια|tr=empatheia|lang=grc|lit=passion|sc=polytonic}} (formed from {{term|ἐν|tr=en-||in, at|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}} + {{term|πάθος|tr=pathos||feeling|lang=grc}}), coined by [[w:Edward B. Titchener|Edward Bradford Titchener]] to translate German ''[[Einfühlung]]''. The modern Greek word {{Grek|[[εμπάθεια]]}} has an opposite meaning denoting strong negative feelings and prejudice against someone.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|empathies|-}}

# the intellectual identification of the thoughts, feelings, or state of another person
# capacity to understand another person's [[point of view]] or the result of such understanding

